<TopicPage slug="subjects/art-history/expert/attribution-conservation-and-forgery">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Attribution, conservation and forgery are <strong>art history\'s laboratory and courtroom</strong>
    — where connoisseurship meets spectroscopy and every judgement carries legal, financial and
    ethical weight. It is the discipline\'s most falsifiable edge.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the tribunal, the instruments, the forgers, the conservator oath" widget="equationStep" config={forgeryCfg} />

  <h2>Expert-level cases and methods</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The Salvator Mundi saga:</strong> rediscovered, heavily restored, attributed, auctioned at $450M — every stage of the attribution lesson\'s machinery (and its doubt) in one story: the object as evidence, the restoration as creation, the price as theology.</li>
    <li><strong>Provenance research as forensic history:</strong> Nazi-era looting records (the beyond-the-West lesson\'s parallel: colonial-era acquisitions) — databases and archives decide restitution claims; the historian\'s source-criticism lesson runs these cases.</li>
    <li><strong>Scientific dating keeps winning:</strong> thermoluminescence on ceramics (the archaeology lesson\'s clocks), C-14 on canvas fibres, MA-XRF scanning (hidden paintings mapped element by element — the chemistry lesson\'s spectroscopy, gallery edition): attribution increasingly means reading an instrument honestly.</li>
    <li><strong>AI enters the connoisseur\'s room:</strong> stroke-analysis models attribute drawings with documented accuracy — and documented failure modes (the AI lesson\'s evaluation discipline: the tool is evidence, not oracle; the tribunal remains human and accountable).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> the van Meegeren trial records; the National Gallery technical bulletins; the conservation ethics codes (IIC/AIC).</p>
</TopicPage>
